Objective:
To understand and implement SQL aggregate functions (COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX)
along with the GROUP BY and HAVING clauses to summarize, group, and filter grouped
data from database tables
Aggregate Functions
Aggregate functions perform a calculation on a set of values and return a single summary
value. They are commonly used with the GROUP BY clause but can also be used on an entire
table.
Function Description
COUNT() Returns the number of rows (or non-null values)
SUM() Returns the total sum of a numeric column
AVG() Returns the average value of a numeric column
MIN() Returns the smallest value in a column
MAX() Returns the largest value in a column

GROUP BY Clause
The GROUP BY clause groups rows that have the same values in specified columns into
summary rows. It is typically used with aggregate functions to produce one result per group
instead of one result per row.
Syntax:
SELECT column1, AGG_FUNCTION(column2)
FROM table_name
WHERE condition
GROUP BY column1;

HAVING Clause
The HAVING clause filters groups created by GROUP BY based on a condition applied to
an aggregate function. Unlike WHERE, which filters individual rows before grouping,
HAVING filters groups after aggregation.
Syntax:
SELECT column1, AGG_FUNCTION(column2)
FROM table_name
WHERE condition
GROUP BY column1

HAVING aggregate_condition
ORDER BY column1;

WHERE vs HAVING
WHERE HAVING
Filters rows before grouping Filters groups after aggregation
Cannot use aggregate functions Can use aggregate functions
Applied to individual records Applied to grouped records
Executes before GROUP BY Executes after GROUP BY

Logical Order of Execution
FROM → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → SELECT → ORDER BY
