Sample Queries
1 Count total number of employees
SELECT COUNT(*) AS TotalEmployees FROM Employee;
2 Total salary paid by the company
SELECT SUM(Salary) AS TotalSalary FROM Employee;
3 Average salary of employees
SELECT AVG(Salary) AS AverageSalary FROM Employee;
4 Highest and lowest salary
SELECT MAX(Salary) AS HighestSalary, MIN(Salary) AS LowestSalary FROM
Employee;
5 Number of employees in each department (GROUP BY)
SELECT Department, COUNT(*) AS EmpCount
FROM Employee
GROUP BY Department;
6 Average salary department-wise
SELECT Department, AVG(Salary) AS AvgSalary
FROM Employee
GROUP BY Department
ORDER BY AvgSalary DESC;
7 Departments with more than 3 employees (HAVING)
SELECT Department, COUNT(*) AS EmpCount
FROM Employee
GROUP BY Department
HAVING COUNT(*) > 3;.
8 Departments where average salary exceeds 50000
SELECT Department, AVG(Salary) AS AvgSalary
FROM Employee
GROUP BY Department
HAVING AVG(Salary) > 50000;
9 Combining WHERE, GROUP BY and HAVING
SELECT Department, City, COUNT(*) AS EmpCount
FROM Employee
WHERE Salary > 40000
GROUP BY Department, City
HAVING COUNT(*) >= 2
ORDER BY Department;

10 City-wise total salary expenditure (only cities spending above 1,00,000)
SELECT City, SUM(Salary) AS TotalSalary
FROM Employee
GROUP BY City
HAVING SUM(Salary) > 100000;

Exercise:
Using the Employee table above (or a table of your own with similar structure), write
SQL queries for the following:
1. Find the number of distinct designations in the company.
2. Find the maximum salary drawn in each department.
3. List departments having an average salary less than 50000.
4. Find the number of employees joined each year (using DOJ).
5. Display department-wise employee count only for departments located in more than one
city.
6. Find the city with the highest total salary expenditure.
7. List designations having more than 2 employees, sorted by count in descending order.

Scenario Based questions
College Result Management System
A college maintains a table Result(StudentID, StudentName, Department, Semester, Subject,
Marks).
1. Find the average marks scored by students in each department.
2. Identify departments where the average marks are below 60, so that remedial classes can
be arranged.
3. Find the number of students who appeared for each subject.
4. List subjects where more than 20 students scored above 80 marks.
5. Find the topper's marks (maximum) in each subject, department-wise.
6. The exam cell wants to know which semester has the lowest overall average marks,
considering only semesters with more than 100 recorded results.
